A||||24||Synth0id||WHAT!?!?! NO POWER STEERING?||||4||
Z||000000||Synth0id||08-07-2000||01:27 PM||||Hey all,<P>I dont have a Fiero yet....but im lookin seriously into gettin one. Actaully im thinkin of getting the FieroGT with 70k miles on it for $3500, this dealership has it for sale...so i figured id take a look. But anyways on to the what i was gonna talk about. I have realized that there is NO POWER STEERING?!?! I actaully knew this before but it finall hit me that this could be a major problem for me.....can any of u guys tell me what its like with out it? Is it hard as hell to turn the wheel? <P>Ur imput would be greatly appreciated...thanx!!<P>Synth0id||||reg||4
Z||000001||Old Lar||08-07-2000||01:55 PM||||When you are not moving the pre 88's are a little stiff.  But once you are moving you get to feel the road.  The steering is quick so you don't need to turn it much unless you are on a road race course.  Then you'll get a work out.<P>With the modifications to the suspension for the 88 models ther is a noticable improvement in the ease of steering. So if you are into paralel parking, you may not enjoy the lack of power steering, but otherwise it's no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||08-07-2000||02:27 PM||||One of the things that attracks me in the Fiero is the fact is hasn't got all those things like power-steering, ABS, etc. It gives you the feeling you are driving a <B>real</B> car instead of those drive-by-wire things they call modern cars...<P>------------------<BR><I>"Auntie Em. Hate you, hate Kansas, taking the dog. Dorothy."</I>||||reg||7
Z||000003||tgowens||08-07-2000||02:51 PM||||Synth0id, just so you know and don't feel that it is a bad deal - none of the Fieros were equipped with power steering - not an option -- sounds like a good car -- talk 'em down tho!! -- and, welcome to the Forum!!!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/MY_SIGNATURE.gif">               <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||08-07-2000||03:12 PM||||Hey, people pay money for a good "pump" at the gym. Just think... a workout and sproty drive all in one package!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Seriously, the best way to tell if you think no power steering will be a problem is to drive the car. Take it into a parking lot and pull in and out of a space. Maybe even try to parallel park (a lost art). You will be surprised how little effort it really takes. <P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000005||Synth0id||08-07-2000||03:12 PM||||Thanx for ur input all,<P><BR>....but man....no power steering. i dont wanna be wrenching the steering wheel around when im tryin to take a corner....and this is all the money i got. im also only 17...so im not a body builder....hmmmm quiet a predicament.....i was soooo excited too. But WAIT!! theres still a chance...when i test it on wednesday. Can u guys tell me whether its hard to shift and steer around corners or what....im probly not gonna be doin a ton of highway drivin....yet atleast...so i need an honest to DOG answer...do u guys think this is a good second car?<P>Thanx again,<P>Synth0id||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||08-07-2000||03:28 PM||||<BR>The Fiero makes an awesome second car, or an awesome primary driver.<P>However, Fieros are older cars and have their down time, therefore be prepared to do some maintenance.  If you are not yet mechanically inclined, read this forum and you will be.<P>Additionally, and please don't take this the wrong way, the problem you see with no PS is, to me at least, an imaginary problem.  The Fiero is small and nimble and responds very well. No offense to my wife, but if she at 5'4" can drive it and not complain, then any man can drive a Fiero, irrespecitve of driving age.<P>I have a 86 four-speed V6 SE and I throw it around corners all the time and have a blast doing it.  Additionally beware, when you purchase a Fiero that you need to give yourself some break in time behind the wheel.   Fieros handle quite differently than most production autos.<P>As for the Fiero, what year is it? What options does it have? How is the overall (inside and outside) condition of the Fiero?<P>These are cool cars and a bunch of fun to drive, but depending on where you live you might find a better deal than on the dealer lot.  If you must purchase this Fiero, I agree with tgownens - talk him down.  Also, check your local classifieds, and check the net to compare prices of similiar optioned Fieros.<P>At last, welcome to PFF and good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 08-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Synth0id||08-07-2000||03:50 PM||||Hey again,<P>What my car has is posted at the very top...and dont worry i certainly will try and talk em down...<P>One last thing....what is turning with one hand like...for example while shifting around a corner....<P>Synth0id||||reg||1
Z||000008||peterh||08-07-2000||03:54 PM||||One thing to help improve the ease of steering is make sure the tires are properly inflated, especially the front ones. The front end is pretty light on these cars so the steering effort is not bad, just not the same as power steering.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroWife||08-07-2000||03:59 PM||||Hey there...<BR>I too am a woman, 5'4" and I have no problem not having power steering.  I don't even notice it anymore.  Our Fiero is a 5-speed and I can park in small spaces, and even parallel park with no problems at all.  Please don't let that be an issue.  When you drive it, you'll see.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/fierowife/Signature.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000010||Synth0id||08-07-2000||04:05 PM||||Well thanx guys...i think im gonna go and look at that 87 gt.  As for power steering...who needs it! Plus i can always go and get the PS kit if i absolutly had to have power steering. but i doubt that will come up. <P>Thanx,<BR>and once again my insane excitement about Fiero's has returned!!<P><BR>Synth0id||||reg||14
Z||000011||tgowens||08-07-2000||04:10 PM||||as much as I hate to admit it, I drive with one hand most of the time too - turning a corner with one hand is no problem -- remember too, that turning a car while moving is much easier than turning the wheels while sitting still -- believe me, you will have NO problem -- and if it IS a problem, let me know and I'll be glad to come out and buy that Fiero!!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/MY_SIGNATURE.gif">               <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Pontiaddict||08-07-2000||04:11 PM||||It's easy to steer with one hand while the car is moving. As a matter of fact, I've overcompensated when I have one hand on the wheel and check my blind spot while merging onto the freeway.(Thhe blind spot is the real problem.) It takes a little more work at a dead stop but all you have to do is roll a little and it's fine. You'll usually never have to crank the wheel off from a dead stop anyway.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 08-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||stimpy||08-07-2000||07:50 PM||||Power Steering Kit?! Uhhh, didn't get the memo on that one.<P>A Little tip on taking a corner. clutch in and downshift before entering the corner, keep clutch in and use momentum, brakes (and both hands) to get you through corner. Upon passing the apex of the turn,clutch out and gas it! Oh what fun! Trust me, you'll dig it. Just make sure it's got all six cylinders!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||b lo 0||08-08-2000||09:31 AM||||I'm 18, and my '85 GT is my daily driver (along with my Wrangler) and I LOVE this car.  Like the others say, the steering is SO direct.  The car tells you exactly what's going on 10 inches under your butt.  As far as wrenching it around a turn, the only time I miss power steering is parallell parking.  I've really learned to do without.  In fact, because the motor is in the back you can hardly tell the difference at anything faster than 15 mph.  Have fun with your GT-btw, good deal!<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">     <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!<P><A HREF="http://clubs.excite.com/welcome/main?cid=.zXb1DJ1lsCL&aid=22&auth=" TARGET=_blank>Pontiac Fiero Owner's Club</A>-Pics, Videos and More-Or Post your Own||||reg||1
Z||000015||Taijiguy||08-08-2000||10:32 AM||||Geeze b lo 0, 10 inches from the seat of your pants???? <BR>What're you driving? A 4 x 4???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  (coming from a guy whos little sled drags speed bumps!)<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000016||Philphine||08-08-2000||11:11 AM||||feels a little clumsy in parking lots but i wouldn't worry about that (i hate people who try to rush around parking lots any way). i also have pretty wide tires in front (225/50-16), and a 5 speed tranny, normal inner city driving is no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||08-08-2000||11:13 AM||||Stimpy, <P>I think the power steering retrofit kit comes with the Lotus suspension upgrade kit...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>Synth, <P>Once the car is rolling, you wont even notice you dont have power steering. Its hard to explain until you've driven one, but you will notice that you are "one with the road" after a quick test drive.  Drive it, You'll like it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dillon - Titusville, FL<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"Matched Perfect and Staggered Special"||||reg||1
