Z||000020||DaRkLoRD||08-08-2000||04:25 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/001719.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/001719.html</A> <P>there's some discussion in that thread about power steering modifications for the Fiero.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A> <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000021||ur2biteme||08-09-2000||02:52 AM||||i'd definitely have to agree with Gold-86SE on this one.  break in time is a MUST.  my first two cars were an 89 ram-50 and a 94 mustang (i miss that car) and that mid engine layout took me over a year to figure out!  my worn suspension probably doesnt help, but Big-O is helping me with a shopping list (thanks a bunch). <P>All i've got is the 4 banger and it's still great.  paralell parking is a pain, not only for the power steering and 5 speed sake, but the rear wheels being near the EXACT corners of the car still messes with me, even after putting 50k on this car in the short time i've had it.  <P>be ready to learn how to turn wrenches though, being that the newest of these cars are 12 years old.  hey, you might turn up a new hobby like me.  i'm still gonna need help in a year with the 3.4......<P>check the car closely though.  i got taken on mine because of circumstance.  check the tires for uneven wear and look for things that are TOO clean.  one thing i've noticed about these cars is that looks are EXTREMELY deceptive.  these plastic bodies weather well, but the car could have had an abusive driver who maintained it badly but kept a good wax job on it.  <P>like i said, check uneven wear on the tires, check underneath and in the engine compartment for things being too clean (masking a problem) or dirty (obvious problems), any leaks, and make sure the check engine light stays out after you start the car.  <P>I may have posted stuff you allready know, but i've been had before and dont want someone else to suffer the same fate.  go for it dude!  even with my busted knuckles this has been the most fun car to drive that i've ever driven.  <P>sorry for the leingth<BR><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>165k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000022||fierospeeder||08-09-2000||04:55 AM||||The only time the steering is hard, is when the car isn't moving.  But the front end is very light because there is no engine in the front.   Parking and some stop and turn around manuevers might be little hard.  Once your moving, its almost as easy as powersteering but you have that go kart feel of the road.  <P>I own a tbird also, and a beetle which doesn't have power steering.   Turn off the power on the tbird, the steering is very difficult because of the weight in the front.  If your tbird, or other front engine car dies and you have to push move it.  It is very hard to do it on your own.<P>Take a test drive, dont be afraid at all.  Try a stick shift 6 cylinder and you will notice how fast and fun it is to drive.  <P>The only bad thing about the fiero is, it is a 16-12 year old car.  You are buying a used car and there will be some things that might go wrong.  This is expectable in all cars over 10 years old and with 100,000 miles.  If your fiero does break down, dont blame the car, the engine in your fiero is in many other GM vehicles and all those cars also break down.  <P>But go out there and take the test drive.  If the car is in bad shape, try another.||||reg||1
Z||000023||fldevel||08-09-2000||04:29 PM||||remember that this is a mid-engined car and all the weight is pretty much tword the rear of the car .. the car is already verry responsive and almost effortless to steer i would imagine that if you put power steering on it the car would be almost uncontrolible ||||reg||1
Z||000024||filthyscarecrow||08-10-2000||03:04 AM||||well, i've had about 5 years experience driving them (i'm 21) adn i  think i'm pretty fast by now. and if shifting while turning is a problem, HEEL AND TOE. that's how racers do it. here's how it goes: approaching a corner, right foot half on the brakes, half above the gas. clutch in. car in neutral, clutch out. clutch in, can in gear, blip throttle with right side of right foot (still braking) clutch out. all before you ever get to the corner, this way you use the throttle to balance the car through the corner. back end starts to slide, give it some gas (this is correct). yuo'd think you would brake to correct oversteer, but with the rear weight bais, throttling it actually transfers weight to the rear wheels and makes it stick. <BR>   the heel and toe i described earlier is double clutching. you don't have to double clutch a fiero as it has synchronizers in the gearbox. normal heel and toe is just clutch in, downshift, blip throttle with right side of foot while braking, clutch out when RPMs are matched, turn. braking should remain smooth and constant throughout. it is messy the first few times you try it, but once you get the hang of it you can drive faster than anyone who doesn't do it. its' also easier on your drivetrain, clutch adn brakes as rpms are matched when everything goes together so there is no jerking, slipping of the clutch (the clutch shouldn't slip once you're moving anyway) and the engine assists in braking, also preventing the rear wheels from locking. it's especially helpful in turbo cars because the blipping of the throttle keeps the turbo spooled up. trust me, i made a guy in a 944 cry by driving this way when he couldn't keep up with me. poor sod. anyway, that's how it's done. <P>------------------<BR>me go fast.||||reg||14
